<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="NutManager.App.Presentation.Controls.NutStatusLed"
             x:Name="Root"
             Width="32"
             Height="32"
             ClipToBounds="False">
  <!--
    One solid ball with two blurred shadows. The ambient shadow keeps the current state legible at
    every point in the cycle; the second shadow expands outwards and disappears like a soft light
    wave. The core never fades or moves, and intentionally has no white lens highlight.

    A shadow colour cannot come from a brush binding, so each state carries its own class. The halo
    is a bare border with no fill - only its strongly blurred shadow is ever visible. The wave uses
    no spread at all: a spread draws a solid band before the blur begins, and once the wave expands
    that band reads as a drawn ring rather than as light. Blur alone has no edge to find. Both
    scale and opacity run on the render thread through the Composition API, with no UI timer.

    Sizes are even numbers inside an even cell so every layer centres on a whole pixel. A
    fractional size leaves the circle straddling a half pixel and the antialiasing comes out
    heavier on one side, which at this diameter reads as the ball sitting crooked.

    The cell stays 32px for an 8px ball and every layer opts out of clipping, allowing the expanded
    blur to dissipate naturally instead of being cut into a square. The extra width is empty space,
    not a larger indicator.

    Colour and animation are decorative; the state is always spelled out in the adjacent text and
    in the accessible name.
  -->
  <UserControl.Styles>
    <Style Selector="Border.nut-led-halo">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="999" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      The travelling ring. Its outline is what moves, so a scale animation carries it outwards through
      the ambient glow instead of inflating a blob — a filled shadow grows with the element and reads as
      the glow swelling, which is a different gesture entirely.

      Blurred, so the outline never reads as a drawn circle. The stroke does thicken as it expands,
      because a scale transform scales everything, and that is fine: a wave spreading out is expected to
      soften rather than stay a hairline.
    -->
    <Style Selector="Border.nut-led-ring">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="999" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1.4" />
      <Setter Property="Effect" Value="blur(2.5)" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.nut-led-ring.healthy">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="#FF3BEF88" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.nut-led-ring.pending">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="#FFFBBF24" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.nut-led-ring.critical">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="#FFF87171" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.nut-led-halo.healthy">
      <!-- Matches NutLedHealthyBrush; a shadow colour cannot be bound to a resource. -->
      <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="0 0 18 0 #FF3BEF88" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.nut-led-halo.pending">
      <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="0 0 18 0 #FFFBBF24" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.nut-led-halo.critical">
      <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="0 0 21 0 #FFF87171" />
    </Style>
  </UserControl.Styles>

  <Grid Width="32" Height="32" Background="Transparent" ClipToBounds="False">
    <!--
      The ambient layer dips and returns when the state changes. That fade is doing two jobs: it is the
      transition in its own right, and it covers the one thing that cannot be tweened — the halo's
      shadow colour lives in a style class and swaps instantly, so it swaps while the layer carrying it
      is at its dimmest.
    -->
    <Border x:Name="AmbientHalo"
            Classes="nut-led-halo"
            Width="6"
            Height="6"
            Opacity="0.76"
            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
            VerticalAlignment="Center"
            IsHitTestVisible="False">
      <Border.Transitions>
        <Transitions>
          <DoubleTransition Property="Opacity" Duration="0:0:0.42" Easing="SineEaseInOut" />
        </Transitions>
      </Border.Transitions>
    </Border>
    <Border x:Name="Halo"
            Classes="nut-led-ring"
            Width="11"
            Height="11"
            Opacity="0"
            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
            VerticalAlignment="Center"
            IsHitTestVisible="False" />
    <!--
      The core crossfades between states instead of cutting. Green to red is the change an operator is
      most likely to catch out of the corner of their eye, and a hard cut there reads as a glitch —
      something redrew — where a fade reads as the thing itself changing.

      BrushTransition interpolates between solid brushes and switches outright between anything else,
      which is why every state brush here is a solid colour.
    -->
    <Ellipse x:Name="Core"
             Width="8"
             Height="8"
             Fill="{Binding StateBrush, ElementName=Root}"
             HorizontalAlignment="Center"
             VerticalAlignment="Center">
      <Ellipse.Transitions>
        <Transitions>
          <BrushTransition Property="Fill" Duration="0:0:0.42" />
        </Transitions>
      </Ellipse.Transitions>
    </Ellipse>
  </Grid>
</UserControl>
